# Presentation

A value keeps its type until it is formatted. A **presentation format** is a
type of text with a known syntax, a subtype of `String`; formatting writes a
value as text of one format, and the result is ordinary text a later step may
read. A **renderer** writes values of one type in one format. The design is
[HQL-0017](../../proposals/HQL-0017/README.md).

## Presentation formats

| Format | Supertype | Text |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Text` | `String` | plain text |
| `Markdown` | `String` | CommonMark |
| `Hmd` | `Markdown` | HyperMarkDown |
| `Json` | `String` | JSON |
| `Yaml` | `String` | YAML |
| `Csv` | `String` | RFC 4180 rows |
| `D2` | `String` | D2 diagram source |
| `Mermaid` | `String` | Mermaid diagram source |

A value of a format type is text that parses in that format. The declarations
are `std/format.hql`.

## `format` and its shorthands

```hql
format<T, F : String>(x: T, as: Type<F>, limit: Int = 0, renderer: String = "") : F
```

`format` is pure and not terminal: its result type is the format, so a step
after it is checked like any other.

- `limit`, when positive, keeps the first `limit` top-level elements — items
  of a list, hits of a ranking, nodes of a graph — and the text marks how many
  were cut.
- `renderer` names the renderer for this call, over the configured one.
- A type and format no renderer writes is a check error naming both. `D2` and
  `Mermaid` describe graphs, so only a `Graph` is written in them.
- Over text already in a format `F`: `format(F)` and `format(Text)` are the
  identity, `Markdown` and `Hmd` widen into each other and copy nothing, and
  any other format written as `Markdown` or `Hmd` is a fenced block with that
  format's info string. No other conversion between formats exists.

Each shorthand fixes the format and nothing else; an option is written in the
longhand, `format(Markdown, limit = 50)`.

| Shorthand | Longhand |
| --- | --- |
| `text` | `format(Text)` |
| `markdown` | `format(Markdown)` |
| `hmd` | `format(Hmd)` |
| `json` | `format(Json)` |
| `yaml` | `format(Yaml)` |
| `csv` | `format(Csv)` |
| `d2` | `format(D2)` |
| `mermaid` | `format(Mermaid)` |

```hql
cards
| graph
| format(Markdown, limit = 20)
```

## Renderers

| Renderer | Type | Formats | Writes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `adjacency` | `Graph` | `Text` | one line per node, its edges indented |
| `node-link` | `Graph` | `Json` | the Cytoscape elements form: `nodes` and `edges`, each under `data` |
| `d2` | `Graph` | `D2`, `Markdown`, `Hmd` | D2 source; in `Markdown`, fenced as `d2` |
| `mermaid` | `Graph` | `Mermaid`, `Markdown`, `Hmd` | a Mermaid `graph` block; in `Markdown`, fenced as `mermaid` |
| `aligned` | any | `Text` | space-aligned columns |
| `pipe` | any | `Markdown`, `Hmd` | a pipe table with header and delimiter rows |
| `rows` | any | `Csv` | rows, header first |
| `records` | any | `Json` | the value's data |
| `list` | any | `Markdown`, `Hmd` | the value's own Markdown rendering |

A `Graph` defaults to `adjacency` in `Text`, `node-link` in `Json`, `d2` in
`Markdown`, `Hmd` and `D2`, and `mermaid` in `Mermaid`. Any other value is
written in `Text` and `Markdown` as its own rendering, in `Json` and `Yaml` as
its data, and in `Csv` as rows.

A vault chooses a renderer per type and format in `hql.toml`; a value whose
type has no entry takes the entry of its nearest supertype, then the default:

```toml
[format.Graph]
markdown = "mermaid"
```

A renderer or format the binary does not have, or a renderer named for a type
or format it does not write, fails loading the vault.

## A result that is not formatted

A host writes a result that is not of a format type in a presentation context:
a terminal writes it as `Text`. [[hql/knowledge|Knowledge]] has no default,
because its views are equally valid and picking one silently would make a
presentation choice look like a property of the knowledge.

Rendering belongs to the host. Printing or saving is an explicit effect, and
constructing an updated [[card-type|Card]] does not itself persist a document.

See [[pipes]] for the execution direction and [[hql/knowledge]] for the
projection this separates itself from.
